A potential ethical issue could be ownership and how they spend their money on the team. If a team makes lucrative amounts of money, but the ownership doesn’t spend a lot of that money, you could argue the owner is taking advantage of the team and the city's loyalty as a way to simply boost their own profits.

The biggest limitation is that recency bias is almost guaranteed any time you explore the data. For example, Coors Field, home of the Colorado Rockies, should be one of the most offense-heavy parks. But the team has been well below average for the past several years. There is also no guarantee that a player will perform at their expected level. All players are subject to a down year where their stats regress a significant amount, which can cause a ripple effect into countless other statistics. Stats that affect the player as an individual include OPS, Hits, strikeouts, exit velo, launch angle, swing speed, or arm strength. Stats that affect the team as a whole can include RBI, WAR, DRS, Home Runs, AVG with RISP.
